# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-2.logic.r2 has "enters → regulated" and "regulated → ¬exempt"; locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 have "enters → regulated" and "¬(enters ∧ regulated ∧ exempt)"; they differ when only "the property of his land is to be regulated by the laws of the society" and "his land is exempt from the jurisdiction of that government, to which he himself, the proprietor, is a subject" are true, which the second allows
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the property of his land is to be regulated by the laws of the society" and "his land is exempt from the jurisdiction of that government, to which he himself, the proprietor, is a subject" are true and "a man incorporates himself into a commonwealth, entering society with others for the securing and regulating of property" is false. Is S3, "To understand this the better, it is fit to consider, that every man, when he at first incorporates himself into any commonwealth, he, by his uniting himself thereunto, annexed also, and submits to the community, those possessions, which he has, or shall acquire, that do not already belong to any other government: for it would be a direct contradiction, for any one to enter into society with others for the securing and regulating of property; and yet to suppose his land, whose property is to be regulated by the laws of the society, should be exempt from the jurisdiction of that government, to which he himself, the proprietor of the land, is a subject.", true then?
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-2.logic.r2 has "land_subject ∧ enjoys → submits"; locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "his possessions are united to the commonwealth and subject to its government and dominion as long as it hath a being" and "someone thenceforth, by inheritance, purchase, permission or otherways, enjoys any part of the land so annexed" are true, which the second allows
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-2.logic.r2, to: Suppose "his possessions are united to the commonwealth and subject to its government and dominion as long as it hath a being" and "someone thenceforth, by inheritance, purchase, permission or otherways, enjoys any part of the land so annexed" are true and "whoever enjoys that land takes it on the condition of submitting to the government of the commonwealth under whose jurisdiction it is, as far forth as any subject of it" is false. Is S5, "Whoever therefore, from thenceforth, by inheritance, purchase, permission, or otherways, enjoys any part of the land, so annexed to, and under the government of that commonwealth, must take it with the condition it is under; that is, of submitting to the government of the commonwealth, under whose jurisdiction it is, as far forth as any subject of it.", true then?
locke-treatise-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: locke-treatise-2.logic.r2 has "¬exempt → land_subject"; locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 has "regulated ∧ ¬exempt → land_subject" and "land_subject → (enjoys → submits)"; they differ when only "the property of his land is to be regulated by the laws of the society", "his land is exempt from the jurisdiction of that government, to which he himself, the proprietor, is a subject", "his possessions are united to the commonwealth and subject to its government and dominion as long as it hath a being" and "someone thenceforth, by inheritance, purchase, permission or otherways, enjoys any part of the land so annexed" are true, which the first allows; locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 has "¬exempt → land_subject" and "land_subject ∧ enjoys → submits" and "enters"
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-2.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 have "enters → (enjoys → submits)"; locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 has "enjoys → submits"; they differ when only "someone thenceforth, by inheritance, purchase, permission or otherways, enjoys any part of the land so annexed" is true, which the first allows
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "someone thenceforth, by inheritance, purchase, permission or otherways, enjoys any part of the land so annexed" is true and "a man incorporates himself into a commonwealth, entering society with others for the securing and regulating of property" and "whoever enjoys that land takes it on the condition of submitting to the government of the commonwealth under whose jurisdiction it is, as far forth as any subject of it" are false. Is the conclusion, "Whoever therefore, from thenceforth, by inheritance, purchase, permission, or otherways, enjoys any part of the land, so annexed to, and under the government of that commonwealth, must take it with the condition it is under; that is, of submitting to the government of the commonwealth, under whose jurisdiction it is, as far forth as any subject of it.", true then?
locke-treatise-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: locke-treatise-2.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if it is not so that his land is exempt from the jurisdiction of that government, to which he himself, the proprietor, is a subject, then his possessions are united to the commonwealth and subject to its government and dominion as long as it hath a being"; locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 does not
locke-treatise-2.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps locke-treatise-2.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if it is not so that his land is exempt from the jurisdiction of that government, to which he himself, the proprietor, is a subject, then his possessions are united to the commonwealth and subject to its government and dominion as long as it hath a being"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
locke-treatise-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 takes for granted "a man incorporates himself into a commonwealth, entering society with others for the securing and regulating of property"; locke-treatise-2.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 do not
locke-treatise-2.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps locke-treatise-2.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "a man incorporates himself into a commonwealth, entering society with others for the securing and regulating of property"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
